{{also|limn-}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|luminem}}, {{m|enm|limnen||to illuminate (a manuscript)}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{m|frm|enluminer}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|illūminō||I illuminate}} (English {{m|en|illuminate}}), {{m|la|in}} + {{m|la|lūminō||light up}}, from {{m|en|lumen|lūmen|light}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*lewk-}} (English {{m|en|leam||glow}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|lĭm}}, {{IPA|/lɪm/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɪm|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|lang=en|lim|limb}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[draw]] or [[paint]]; [[delineate]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[describe]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[illuminate]], as a manuscript.

====Usage notes====
* Particularly used in arts journalism; not understood by significant portion of American public.<ref>[http://www.baltimoresun.com/news/maryland/bs-md-limn-headline-20100907,0,3496485.story The Sun goes out on a limn with unusual headline: Unusual word in front-page headline leaves some readers scratching their heads], Jill Rosen, ''The Baltimore Sun,'' September 7, 2010</ref>

